074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & =13\\,220-\\frac{1}{28}(588)^{2} \\\\  & =13\\,220-12\\,348 \\\\  & =872.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two sets of $18$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{18},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{18}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=36,\\,{S}_{yy}=27,\\,{S}_{xy}=30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${\\sigma }_{y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f1ce794-6e38-5896-94a0-a08e42af5009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512890 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the variance of ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{yy}$ is the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}.$\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{yy}=27.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512901 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512901 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512901 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref